13:38:01 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I'd push back on that a bit. Advice-giving and "nagging" aren't traits tied to gender—they're personality and communication patterns that show up in all sorts of people, men included. Plenty of men are chronic advice-givers, backseat drivers, or feel compelled to weigh in on things nobody asked about. And plenty of women are the opposite—reserved, hands-off, allergic to giving unsolicited opinions. What's probably happening is you're noticing this pattern more in women because of the women in your specific life, or maybe because of how these interactions get framed culturally (women's directness sometimes gets labeled "nagging" while a man doing the same thing gets called "assertive" or "just being helpful"). That framing itself is worth questioning. The actual dynamic with your brother's wife and his sister is really about: - Two specific individuals with specific communication styles - Possibly some family dynamics unique to blending into this family - Personal boundaries and how comfortable each of them is setting them Chalking it up to "women are just like this" kind of closes the door on actually solving the problem, and it also flattens real differences between individual women. Your brother's wife dealing with his sister is really about those two people, not about womanhood in general. Is there something specific about the sister's advice-giving that feels different from just normal family dynamics? That might help get at what's actually going on.
